### Module controls

Every module should support:

- Add, edit, duplicate, reorder, collapse and expand.
- Set a title, description and sequence.
- Show lesson count and estimated duration.
- Move lessons between modules.
- Archive or delete with confirmation and dependency checks.
- Control whether its content is available to students, while respecting individual lesson permissions.

### Lesson controls

Every lesson should support:

- Title, description, type and estimated duration.
- Rich-text content, video, code examples, images, files or assessments.
- Free-preview or enrolled-student access.
- Draft, published and scheduled states.
- Preview, duplicate, reorder and delete actions.
- Completion requirements and optional prerequisites.

The builder must preserve unsaved work, handle network errors and clearly report when a save succeeds or fails.

## 5. Free and paid access rules

The system should distinguish the course's commercial price from the access setting of each lesson.

| Course configuration           | Student experience                                                                      |
| ------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Free course                    | Enroll for free; access all published course content.                                   |
| Paid course                    | View course details and curriculum; purchase before accessing protected content.        |
| Paid course with free previews | Access designated preview lessons; purchase to unlock premium content.                  |
| Paid course already purchased  | Access all content included in the enrollment, subject to publication and course rules. |
| Unpublished course             | Not publicly available; authorized administrators can preview it.                       |

For individual lessons, the administrator should be able to choose:

- Free preview: accessible without buying the course, subject to any required account rules.
- Enrolled students: accessible to students with a valid enrollment.
- Prerequisite required: accessible only when the defined learning conditions are satisfied.

The system should never rely on a frontend lock icon alone. Lesson APIs, video delivery and protected file downloads must independently verify access on the server.

A course-wide free setting should normally override the purchase requirement, but prerequisite and publication rules can still apply. This avoids confusing students with unexpected paywalls inside a free course.

## 6. Student experience — from discovery to completion

### A. Course catalog

Students should see a searchable course catalog with a professional card for every published course.

Each card should show:

- Course thumbnail and title.
- Short description and instructor.
- Beginner, intermediate or advanced level.
- Course duration and number of lessons.
- Free or paid pricing.
- Enrollment status or learning progress.
- A clear action: View course, Start learning or Continue learning.

Filters can include category, level, price, duration and learning status. Draft courses must never appear in public results.

### B. Course detail page

The detail page should explain the course before a student enrolls or pays.

