The course player should support:

- Curriculum sidebar with module expansion and lesson status.
- Video playback and reading progress where applicable.
- Lesson resources and downloadable attachments.
- Previous and next navigation.
- Mark complete or automatic completion rules.
- Quiz attempts, scores and feedback.
- Resume from the last lesson.
- Mobile-friendly navigation.
- Clear locked-content explanations and purchase links where appropriate.

The percentage displayed must be calculated from the defined completion policy, not hardcoded or inferred only from how much of a video was watched.

## 7. Student progress and course completion

Each student's learning state should be saved independently for each enrollment.

A useful initial progress model is:

- Not started.
- In progress.
- Completed.
- Locked because of access or prerequisites.

The platform should track the last opened lesson, lesson completion timestamps, quiz results, assessment attempts and overall course progress where relevant.

A course can be marked complete when all required lessons and assessments meet the course's configured completion rules. Optional lessons should not block completion unless the course explicitly requires them.

Certificates should be optional and issued only when the configured completion conditions are satisfied.

## 8. Recommended data model

Use a normalized relational structure if the existing project uses a relational database. Adapt the names and conventions to the current schema rather than introducing a competing system.

| Entity             | Responsibility                                                                   |
| ------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `courses`          | Course details, slug, instructor, publication state and pricing mode.            |
| `course_modules`   | Course association, title, description and module order.                         |
| `lessons`          | Module association, title, type, content reference, order and publication state. |
| `lesson_resources` | Files, attachments and supporting resources.                                     |
| `enrollments`      | Student, course, status, enrollment source and access validity.                  |
| `orders`           | Purchases, currency, amount and payment state.                                   |
| `payments`         | Provider reference, verification status and payment events.                      |
| `lesson_progress`  | Student enrollment, lesson status and completion timestamps.                     |
| `quiz_attempts`    | Assessment attempts, scores and results.                                         |
| `certificates`     | Completion records and certificate identifiers, if enabled.                      |

Important relationships:

- One course has many modules.
- One module has many lessons.
- One lesson can have many resources.
- One student can have many enrollments.
- One enrollment belongs to one course and student.
- One enrollment can have progress records for multiple lessons.
- An order can have payment attempts or provider events, with idempotent handling.
- Course content and access policies must remain separate from student progress.

Use foreign keys, unique constraints, transactions and appropriate indexes. For example, enforce a unique enrollment for each student/course pair where the product's enrollment rules require it.

Do not store entire uploaded videos as database blobs by default. Store files in an appropriate object-storage service and keep their metadata and protected storage references in the database.

## 9. API and backend architecture

The feature should use the existing authentication, database, payment integrations and API conventions wherever possible. Do not create duplicate infrastructure just for the academy.

A possible API design, subject to the existing architecture:

| Method  | Endpoint                     | Purpose                                                            |
| ------- | ---------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `GET`   | `/courses`                   | List published courses                                             |
| `GET`   | `/courses/:slug`             | View course details and permitted curriculum metadata              |
| `POST`  | `/admin/courses`             | Create a course                                                    |
| `PATCH` | `/admin/courses/:id`         | Update course details                                              |
| `POST`  | `/admin/courses/:id/modules` | Add a module                                                       |
| `POST`  | `/admin/modules/:id/lessons` | Add a lesson                                                       |
| `PATCH` | `/admin/lessons/:id`         | Edit lesson content or access settings                             |
| `POST`  | `/admin/courses/:id/publish` | Validate and publish a course                                      |
| `POST`  | `/courses/:id/enroll`        | Enroll in a free course or initiate the configured enrollment flow |
| `POST`  | `/courses/:id/checkout`      | Create a paid checkout                                             |
| `POST`  | `/payments/webhook`          | Process verified payment-provider events                           |
| `GET`   | `/me/courses`                | List the student's enrollments                                     |
| `GET`   | `/me/courses/:id/progress`   | Read course progress                                               |
| `POST`  | `/me/lessons/:id/complete`   | Record lesson completion                                           |

These are proposed routes, not a claim that they already exist in your project.

Security requirements

- Verify authentication and role permissions on every protected operation.
- Scope student access to their own enrollments and progress.
- Check lesson access on the server before returning protected content.
- Validate uploads, file types, sizes and storage permissions.
- Verify webhook signatures and prevent duplicate processing.
- Prevent unauthorized changes to prices, enrollment status and payment records.
- Use consistent validation and error responses.
- Audit important administrative actions.

## 10. Edge cases the implementation must handle

| Scenario                                  | Expected behavior                                                         |
| ----------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| A lesson is saved as a draft              | It is not accessible to ordinary students.                                |
| A course is unpublished                   | It is removed from public discovery and new enrollment.                   |
| A student directly requests a paid lesson | The server denies access without valid entitlement.                       |
| Payment is still pending                  | Paid content remains locked.                                              |
| Payment webhook is delivered twice        | Only one purchase and corresponding entitlement are created.              |
| A module is reordered                     | Lesson order and student progress remain correct.                         |
| A lesson is deleted                       | The system checks dependencies and preserves required historical records. |
| A paid course changes price               | Existing purchases retain their original transaction records.             |
| A student loses access                    | Future requests follow the configured revocation policy.                  |
| A lesson changes after completion         | Existing progress is handled according to a defined versioning policy.    |
| The network fails during editing          | The user receives a clear save status and can recover unsaved work.       |
| A course has no lessons                   | Publishing is blocked unless the product explicitly allows empty courses. |

## 11. Development plan and acceptance criteria

Build the feature in stages so each part can be tested before the next one depends on it.

Phase 1 — Foundation

Required

Inspect existing architecture, identify reusable components, define schema and permissions, and implement courses, modules and lessons.

Acceptance: administrators can create, edit, reorder and save a complete curriculum.

Phase 2 — Course experience

Implement the course catalog, detail page, course player, lesson editor and responsive layouts.

Acceptance: students can navigate published free courses and complete lessons with saved progress.

Phase 3 — Access and payments

Implement free enrollment, paid checkout, per-lesson previews and server-side authorization.

Acceptance: free, paid, pending-payment and unauthorized-access scenarios pass automated tests.

Phase 4 — Quality and launch

Add assessments, completion rules, optional certificates, monitoring and documentation.

Acceptance: end-to-end tests cover course creation, enrollment, payment, learning and completion.

## 12. Final definition of done

The ICAcademy module is ready for release when:

- An authorized administrator can create and publish a course.
- Each course can contain multiple ordered modules and lessons.
- Lessons support the required content types and individual access rules.
- Free enrollment works without a payment.
- Paid enrollment works only after verified payment.
- Free previews work without unlocking other premium content.
- Students can see their courses and resume learning.
- Progress and assessments persist correctly.
- Course publication, editing, deletion and permissions are enforced.
- The complete flow works on desktop and mobile.
- Automated tests cover the critical paths and failure cases.
- The feature follows the existing product's design system and engineering conventions.

My recommendation: treat this as a full academy subsystem, with the curriculum builder at its center. Build the course hierarchy, access rules and enrollment model first; then connect the admin UI, student experience and payments to that foundation.

One qualification: this is the proposed end-to-end specification, not a verified audit of your current repository. The actual implementation should begin by inspecting the existing project and adapting these requirements to its established architecture, avoiding duplicate routes, tables, components or payment logic.